static/img/figures/gqur-301/unit-08/fig-U8-2.svg: dark label equality True
The permutation formula with school examples
A table of the permutation formula P(n, r) = n! divided by (n - r)! with four school examples: three pupils in a line give 6 orders, a head prefect and deputy from 8 candidates give 56, three prizes among 10 finalists give 720, and the letters of KARACHI give 2,520.
Permutations: P(n, r) = n! ÷ (n - r)!
Situation
Working
Count
3 pupils in an assembly line
3! = 3 × 2 × 1
6
Head prefect and deputy from 8
P(8, 2) = 8 × 7
56
1st, 2nd and 3rd prize from 10 finalists
P(10, 3) = 10 × 9 × 8
720
Letters of KARACHI (two A’s alike)
7! ÷ 2! = 5,040 ÷ 2
2,520
Caption: n! counts all orders, P(n, r) counts orders of r items
Order matters in every row: swapping two people or letters gives a different result.
Start at n and multiply r shrinking factors. If some items are alike, divide by their factorials.
Credit: textbook.com.pk after Abramson (2021).
textbook.com.pk

