static/img/figures/gqur-301/unit-08/fig-U8-4.svg: dark label equality True
The combination formula with school examples
A table of the combination formula C(n, r) = n! divided by r! times (n - r)! with five school examples: a workshop group of 3 from 8 teachers gives 56, two register carriers from 3 volunteers give 3, a cricket eleven from 14 gives 364, any 5 of 8 exam questions gives 56, and handshakes among 10 teachers give 45.
Combinations: C(n, r) = n! ÷ (r! × (n - r)!)
Situation
Working
Count
3 teachers for a workshop, from 8
336 ÷ 3! = 336 ÷ 6
56
2 register carriers, from 3 volunteers
C(3, 2)
3
A cricket XI from a squad of 14
C(14, 11) = C(14, 3)
364
Any 5 of 8 examination questions
C(8, 5) = C(8, 3)
56
Handshakes among 10 teachers
C(10, 2) = 90 ÷ 2
45
Caption: C(n, r) = P(n, r) ÷ r!
Order does not matter in any row: only the group counts.
Choosing r is the same as leaving out n - r, so C(n, r) = C(n, n - r) often shortens the working.
Credit: textbook.com.pk after Abramson (2021).
