static/img/figures/gqur-301/unit-08/fig-U8-5.svg: dark label equality True
Which counting method?
A flowchart for choosing a counting method. If order does not matter, use a combination. If order matters and items can repeat, use the multiplicative rule, n to the power r. If order matters without repetition, use n factorial when all items are arranged, or P(n, r) when only some are. A note says to multiply across stages and add across separate cases.
Which counting method?
Read the problem: what is being chosen,
and from how many?
Does order matter? (swap test)
no
Combination
C(n, r) = n! ÷ (r! (n - r)!)
yes
Can an item be used again?
yes
Multiplicative rule
n × n × ... = n to the power r
no
Are all n items arranged?
yes
n!
no
Permutation
P(n, r) = n! ÷ (n - r)!
Several stages? Count each stage, then multiply.
Separate cases? Count each case, then add.
Caption: two questions choose the method
Swap test: if swapping two chosen items gives a different outcome, order matters.
Then check the answer with a small listed case and a size check.
Credit: textbook.com.pk after Abramson (2021).
textbook.com.pk

static/img/figures/gqur-301/unit-08/fig-U8-6.svg: dark label equality True
Solved school counting problems
A table of six solved school counting problems, each labelled with whether order matters, the method and the answer: a lock code with repeats gives 1,000, three prizes among 10 give 720, a team of 4 from 10 gives 210, 2 boys and 2 girls give 60, a line with two friends together gives 48, and a committee with at least one girl gives 74.
